--- abstract

Message size limits mean that large files are sent by email as links
to a web server rather than as attachments.  A link loses the
properties that make an attachment useful: it can stop working, the
file behind it can change, it can't be scanned in transit, and it may
need a separate login.

This document defines the "Link-Attachment" header field, which lets
receiving systems fetch, verify and keep a linked file, and then treat
it like any other attachment.  It also defines markup that lets aware
clients hide the fallback links in the message body.

--- middle

# Introduction {#intro}

Most SMTP systems limit the size of messages that can be sent, which
can be as low as 10 megabytes.  Moreover, limits vary depending on the
recipient's system.  End users want to send files to each other, and
the files they create keep getting bigger.

The usual workaround is to upload the file to a web server and send a
link.  Many clients and services do this, each in their own way.

An attached file has properties that a link lacks:

1. A copy lasts as long as the message.
2. It can't be changed after sending.
3. Systems that handle the message can scan it.
4. Anyone who has the message has the file.

A link can stop working, the file behind it can change, it can't be
scanned in transit, and it may need a separate login.

This document defines the "Link-Attachment" header field ({{header}}),
which gives the URL, size and digest of each file and, if the file is
encrypted, its key.  Receiving servers fetch, verify and scan the file
when the message arrives ({{receiving}}), and keep it as long as the
message ({{lifetime}}), which restores those properties.  Until
support is widespread, the message body also includes ordinary links,
with markup that lets clients that support this document hide them
({{html}}).

This document replaces {{BIG-FILES-00}}; see {{changes}}.

# Conventions and Definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

This document uses the terms MUA, MDA and message store from
{{MAIL-ARCH}}, and:

sender:
: The sending MUA, or a service acting for it.

linked attachment:
: A file described by a Link-Attachment header field.

attachment server:
: The HTTP {{HTTP}} server that holds a linked attachment.

receiving server:
: Any system on the recipient's side that inspects message content,
  such as an MDA, a content filter or a message store.

# The Link-Attachment Header Field {#header}

Each "Link-Attachment" header field describes one linked attachment.
It appears only in the header section of a message ({{Section 2.1 of
MAIL}}); one in the header fields of a body part MUST be ignored.  It
applies to the message in whose header section it appears, including a
message encapsulated in a message/rfc822 body part ({{Section 5.2.1 of
MEDIA-TYPES}}), and "cid" URLs in that message resolve against its own
Link-Attachment header fields.

The syntax is that of Content-Disposition {{CONTENT-DISP}}, so existing
MIME parsers can read it.  The disposition type is "attachment", or
"inline" for a file that the HTML body shows in place ({{cid-urls}}),
with the same meanings as in {{CONTENT-DISP}}.  Receivers MUST treat
unknown types as "attachment".

## Parameters {#params}

Parameter names are case-insensitive.  Values can use {{MIME-PARAM}}
encoding, for long values and for non-ASCII file names; it is decoded
before the rules below apply, so "filename\*" is the "filename"
parameter.  Unknown parameters MUST be ignored.

A receiver MUST ignore a Link-Attachment header field in which a
parameter defined here appears more than once, a REQUIRED parameter is
missing, or a value is invalid.

cid:
: REQUIRED.  An identifier, with the syntax of id-left "@" id-right
  from {{MAIL}}.  It MUST be unique within the message, MUST NOT equal
  any Content-ID ({{Section 7 of MIME}}) in the message, and SHOULD be
  globally unique.  The HTML body refers to the file by it ({{html}},
  {{cid-urls}}), and it stays the same when the message is forwarded.

url:
: REQUIRED.  An absolute "https" URI {{URI}} for the file
  ({{fetching}}).

size:
: REQUIRED.  The size of the file in octets, after any decryption.

digest-*:
: REQUIRED.  A digest of the file, after any decryption.  The name
  after "digest-" is from the IANA "Hash Algorithms for HTTP Digest
  Fields" registry {{DIGEST-FIELDS}}, and the value is base64
  {{BASE64}}.  "digest-sha-256" MUST be present; others MAY be.
  Deprecated algorithms MUST NOT be used.  Receivers MUST support
  sha-256.

filename:
: RECOMMENDED.  As for Content-Disposition {{CONTENT-DISP}}.

type:
: OPTIONAL.  The media type of the file, without parameters.  The
  default is "application/octet-stream".  It overrides the Content-Type
  of the HTTP response.

key:
: OPTIONAL.  The algorithm and key used to encrypt the stored file
  ({{key-format}}).  If absent, the file is not encrypted.

expires:
: OPTIONAL.  A date-time {{MAIL}} until which the sender intends the
  file to be available.

## Key Format {#key-format}

A key is an algorithm name, a colon, and the key material in base64url
{{BASE64}} without padding:

~~~ abnf
key-value       = enc-alg ":" key-material
enc-alg         = 1*( %x61-7A / DIGIT / "-" )
                  ; content coding name, in lowercase
key-material    = 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_" )
~~~

The algorithm is a content coding from the HTTP Content Coding Registry
({{Section 16.6.1 of HTTP}}) whose specification defines it as
encrypting and says what its key is.  A content coding defines the whole
stored format, not just the cipher, so a receiver can verify a large
file as it streams.

Receivers MUST support "aes128gcm" {{ECE}}.  Senders SHOULD use it
unless they know every recipient supports another.  For "aes128gcm", the
key material is the input keying material of {{Section 2.2 of ECE}}: at
least 16 random octets, and the sender SHOULD leave the "keyid" field
empty.

The attachment server serves the encrypted file without a
Content-Encoding header field, and the receiver decrypts it.  A
receiver that doesn't support the algorithm MUST treat the linked
attachment as unavailable.

## Formal Syntax {#abnf}

This uses ABNF {{ABNF}}.  "token", "value", "attribute" and
"extension-token" are from {{MIME}}; CFWS, quoted-string, id-left,
id-right and date-time are from {{MAIL}}.

~~~ abnf
link-attachment = "Link-Attachment:" [CFWS] la-disposition
                  *([CFWS] ";" [CFWS] la-parameter) [CFWS] CRLF

la-disposition  = "attachment" / "inline" / extension-token

la-parameter    = cid-param / url-param / size-param /
                  digest-param / filename-param / type-param /
                  key-param / expires-param / other-param

cid-param       = "cid" "=" value      ; id-left "@" id-right
url-param       = "url" "=" value      ; absolute "https" URI
size-param      = "size" "=" 1*DIGIT
digest-param    = "digest-" token "=" value   ; base64
filename-param  = "filename" "=" value
type-param      = "type" "=" value     ; type "/" subtype
key-param       = "key" "=" value      ; key-value
expires-param   = "expires" "=" quoted-string ; date-time
other-param     = attribute "=" value
~~~

A URL can't contain whitespace, so whitespace in the value of "url" is
removed before it is used.  This lets a long URL be folded.

## Example {#example-header}

~~~
Link-Attachment: attachment;
    cid="a1.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com";
    filename="site-survey.mp4"; type="video/mp4"; size=734003200;
    url="https://files.example.com/la/
         Hq3Ld9YtW2xKq8vZp0aN5uFbE7cJr1sM";
    digest-sha-256="X48E9qOokqqrvdts8nOJRJN3OWDUoyWxBf7kbu9DBPE=";
    key="aes128gcm:9Mzuq2AvtfQJxwuIzYB4Gg";
    expires="Thu, 17 Dec 2026 09:15:00 +0000"
~~~

# Message Body {#body}

The body SHOULD include, for each linked attachment, a link from which
the file can be downloaded with a web browser, with its file name and
size.  A sender MAY leave the links out when it knows that every
recipient's MUA supports this document, for example because they all
use the same service.

This download link is usually not the "url" parameter, which gives the
stored, possibly encrypted, file.  So that the attachment server can
decrypt the file, the download link SHOULD include the key in the
format of {{key-format}}, for example as a query parameter.  The
attachment server chooses the rest of the URL.  See {{security}}.

The message SHOULD have an HTML {{HTML}} body part.  A text/plain part
SHOULD have the same links, though MUAs can't hide them there.

## HTML Markup {#html}

In the HTML body, the link to each linked attachment SHOULD be inside a
"div" element with class "link-attachment" and an "id" equal to its
"cid".  All of them MAY be inside a "div" element with class
"link-attachments", along with any introductory text.  The example
uses the line wrapping of {{WRAP}}.

~~~ html
=============== NOTE: '\' line wrapping per RFC 8792 ================

<div class="link-attachments">
  <p>Bron Gondwana shared a file with you:</p>
  <div class="link-attachment"
       id="a1.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com">
    <a href="https://dl.example/pR7wQxV3mTz9KcLe2Hd8Fy?k=aes128gcm:\
      9Mzuq2AvtfQJxwuIzYB4Gg">site-survey.mp4</a> (700 MiB)
  </div>
</div>
~~~

An MUA that shows a linked attachment as an attachment SHOULD hide its
"link-attachment" element, and SHOULD hide a "link-attachments" element
once it has hidden every "link-attachment" element inside it.  An MUA
MUST NOT hide a link unless it has a verified copy of the file.

## CID URLs {#cid-urls}

A "cid" URL {{CID}} in the HTML body can refer to a linked attachment,
as it can to a body part, for example:

~~~ html
<img src="cid:a2.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com"
     alt="Site plan">
~~~

An MUA resolves a "cid" URL to the body part with that Content-ID, if
there is one, and otherwise to the linked attachment with that "cid".
It resolves it only to a verified copy of the file ({{fetching}}).  An
MUA that would have to fetch the file from the attachment server to
display it SHOULD treat it as remote content, under the same policy as
remote images ({{security}}).

MUAs that don't support this document can't resolve these URLs, and
will show, for example, a broken image.  Senders SHOULD NOT use them
unless they know that the recipients' MUAs support this document.

# Sending {#sending}

## Publishing the File {#publishing}

The sender stores the file on an attachment server, and chooses its
URL and key.  Unless the file is
meant to be public, the sender SHOULD encrypt it with a random key.

The "url" and the download link MUST NOT be guessable, unless the file
is already public.  Each SHOULD contain at least 128 random bits, as a
capability URL {{CAPABILITY-URLS}}.  Having the message then means
having the file.

The attachment server MUST serve GET requests for the "url" without
authentication, cookies or other state, and MUST always return the same
content for it.  It SHOULD keep the file until "expires", if given, and
for at least several days in any case.  Its download pages SHOULD send
"Referrer-Policy: no-referrer".

## Composing {#composing}

The MUA adds a Link-Attachment header field for each linked attachment,
and links in the body ({{body}}).  A DKIM {{DKIM}} signature SHOULD
cover every Link-Attachment header field, and SHOULD list it once more
in "h=" to prevent more being added.

A linked attachment can be kept in a draft, and is published once.

## Forwarding and Replying {#forwarding}

An MUA that forwards a message with linked attachments, or replies and
keeps them, SHOULD publish its own copy of each file ({{publishing}})
and give the new message Link-Attachment header fields and body links
that point to that copy.  The "cid", "size" and digests stay the same.
This keeps the original attachment server from serving every later
recipient ({{security}}), and the file from depending on it.

When forwarding a message as a message/rfc822 body part, which can't be
changed, the MUA SHOULD add Link-Attachment header fields for its own
copies to the outer message.  Receivers match these to the
encapsulated message's linked attachments by digest, and a receiving
server that has a file with the same digest needn't fetch it again.

# Receiving {#receiving}

## Fetching and Verifying {#fetching}

A receiving server or MUA fetches a linked attachment with a GET
request to its "url".  The client:

* MUST NOT send cookies, credentials or anything else that identifies
  the recipient;

* MUST verify the server's certificate ({{Section 4.3.4 of HTTP}});

* MAY follow redirects, only to "https" URIs, and SHOULD limit how many;

* treats any final status code other than 2xx as failure, and MAY retry
  after a 429 (Too Many Requests) {{RATE-LIMIT}}, a 5xx or a network
  error; and

* SHOULD stop reading once the content is longer than "size" plus the
  encryption overhead, which for "aes128gcm" follows from the record
  size in its header.

A client that retries MUST back off.  After a response with a
Retry-After header field ({{Section 10.2.3 of HTTP}}), it MUST NOT send
another request to that host before the time given.  Otherwise it MUST
wait at least a minute before the first retry, at least double the wait
after each further failure, and add random jitter.  The back-off
applies to the host, not just the URL, so that a receiving server
fetching many files from one attachment server slows down for all of
them.  A client SHOULD give up after a day or so, and treat the linked
attachment as unavailable.

The client then decrypts the file if there is a "key", and checks
"size" and each "digest-*" whose algorithm it supports.  If none is
supported, or any check fails, the linked attachment is unavailable,
and MUST NOT be shown as a verified attachment.

A receiving server MAY decline to fetch a file, for example because it
is too large.  The MUA or the user can then fetch it.

## Content Scanning {#scanning}

A receiving server SHOULD fetch and scan each linked attachment that
it would have scanned if the file had been attached, and apply the
same policy to the result.  If fetching during the SMTP transaction
would take too long, it MAY scan after accepting the message and before
delivering it.

## Storage and Access {#storage}

A message store SHOULD fetch each linked attachment on delivery, if it
wasn't fetched during scanning, and store it with the message.

It SHOULD let the user's MUAs fetch the stored file the same way as an
attached file, so the MUA needn't contact the attachment server.  It
MUST NOT let a user fetch the file unless the user can read a message
that refers to it.  This applies to any lookup by digest too.

{:aside}
> Open issue: This is specific to each access protocol.  JMAP
> {{JMAP-MAIL}} could list linked attachments among an Email's
> attachments, each with a blobId; {{BLOBEXT}} already describes blobs
> by digest.  IMAP would need a FETCH data item.  These might be
> separate documents, along with a way for an MUA to get proxied remote
> images.

An MUA whose message store doesn't do this MAY fetch the file itself,
when the user opens it or in the background on arrival, but SHOULD NOT
fetch it just because the message was displayed, except for an image
that the user's remote image policy allows ({{cid-urls}}).

## Lifetime {#lifetime}

A message store SHOULD keep a linked attachment as long as any message
that refers to it, and MAY then remove it.  It can keep one copy for
many messages, found by digest.

# Requirements and Alternatives Not Taken {#alternatives}

The design meets these requirements:

* MUAs that don't support this document can show links to the files.
* A message can have many linked attachments.
* Linked attachments can be kept in drafts, forwarded and replied to.
* Receiving systems can restore the properties in {{intro}}.

The message/external-body media type {{MEDIA-TYPES}} also refers to
external content, but many MUAs show it as an attachment with no link,
and it has no digest or key.

{{BIG-FILES-00}} put its header field in the body part holding the
fallback links.  This document puts it in the header section of the
message, with the other header fields that servers read and DKIM
signs.

